# Update 56 — smoke testing the release

Upstream `6cd2ca1b`, 2026-09-08, seed `D9CF240465C3D0BC`. U55 (`675a0775`) to
U56 is 874 files, +65,116 / -23,086.

The checkout is a NEW detached worktree `~/showell_repos/cobblestone-u56rel`.
Nothing existing was moved: `u56-candidate` and `u56-candidate-sunday` are both
still parked where they were, and neither is an ancestor of the release --
Damian squashed, so ancestry cannot answer "did our fix land".

**The seed file's sha256 begins `d9cf240465c3d0bc`**, so the seed named in a
release note IS the sha256 prefix of `seed/Codex.cdx`. That is a free identity
check on any checkout and it is worth doing first.

## Our PRs, and how we know they landed

Damian's release note names 117, 118, 119, 127, 128, 129, 130, 131 and 134 as
ingested, with 132 and 133 omitted from the paragraph by mistake and corrected
on their main afterwards. The credit lives in
`docs/PM/Active/GitHubUpdates/GitHubUpdate56.md`, never in the one-line commit
message -- all 56 release commits are one line by convention.

Two probes of our own, and BOTH were broken before they were right:

- **reverse-apply** (`git apply --reverse --check`) said "not verbatim" for all
  eleven. Meaningless: across a 65k-line update the context lines cannot match.
  It is the right probe for "is this branch upstream" and the wrong one for
  "did this change land".
- **content presence** said 0/40 for every PR, contradicting Damian outright.
  Two bugs stacked: `grep` on this box is **ugrep**, where `--exclude-dir=.git`
  warns and makes every `-q` probe return false -- so every lookup was a false
  negative -- and the lines being sampled were backlog PROSE, which upstream
  always rewrites in its own words.

Restricted to code files with a working grep:

    129  25/25 (100%)   130  14/14 (100%)   133  25/25 (100%)
    118  20/25  (80%)   128   9/13  (69%)   132  17/25  (68%)
    131  16/25  (64%)   119   5/11  (45%)   127   3/11  (27%)
    117   3/23  (13%)

Present, adapted rather than copied. **A red from an instrument nobody has
calibrated is not a finding.** Both probes had to be checked against a known
answer before either was worth quoting.

## Two registered predictions now testable

- **`lower-def` emits the stripped type** (COMPILER-55 / our PR 134) landed, so
  "an unrelated name's sort position stops deciding a definition's shape". The
  essay registered the prediction before the measurement: on repin, the
  self-host should go **2,846 of 2,846** byte-identical, up from 2,845.
- **`gen-eq-def` synthesises `__eq_<T>` for every comparable sum, and an
  instantiated helper is minted for a generic sum and attached to the wire.**
  A new synthesised-definition family -- the exact vein the essay called the
  richest, and the shape our counters are most sensitive to.

COMPILER-67 is that same equality arc breaking upstream's own `.codex` printer:
TEXT mode printed the lowered `__eq_TokenKind ...` where the source read `=`,
and it failed 14 definitions at their release gate.


## Smoke test 1 — cobblestone-qemu: does the transport still work?

**What this repo proves:** run Codex on real x86 and tell me what came out.
Four steps, only the last not a VM — bundle (pwsh) → compile through the seed
under QEMU → transpile through the ring plug under QEMU → `zig build-exe`. If
`fib` builds and prints the right numbers, U56's seed boots, its compiler
compiles, and its zig plug emits. It says nothing about anything larger.

**RED first, and it was ours.** `zig_plug_pages.txt` — a hand-kept list of the
pages of `Chapter: Zig Emitter` — named four pages. Upstream carries ONE
(`ZigEmitter.codex`) at both U55 and U56; the four-page split is on our own
`u56-candidate` branch and upstream did not take it. So the bundler asked for
`ZigEmitterExpressions.codex`, pwsh died on a missing file, and it did so
*three steps into a build that had already booted a guest*.

**`check_zig_pages.py` catches this in under a second, and I did not run it.**
That linter exists for exactly this, memory says to run the linters before any
guest, and I booted the guest first anyway. The cost was small here only
because fib is the cheap subject.

Fixed properly rather than by editing the list: `zig_plug_pages.py` reads the
chapter's own `Page N of M` footers out of the checkout and refuses if they do
not describe a whole chapter. Same code gives one page against the release and
four against `u56-candidate`. The list is deleted — it was a second copy of
something the chapter already says, and a second copy goes stale on exactly the
release you most want to measure.

**GREEN.** Against `6cd2ca1b`:

    ring plug   bundled 7442 lines, 390080 bytes, compiled under QEMU
    fib         compile to IR 4s · transpile 3s · zig build 8s
    ./fib       55 / 610          <- fib 10 and fib 15, correct

So U56's seed boots and its whole bare-metal transport is sound.

**It is now `cobblestone-qemu`**, at
`github.com/showell/cobblestone-qemu` — it had no remote at all before this,
having been carved out on 2026-09-04 and never given one, so everything in it
lived on this box alone. The repo takes the name of the thing it runs; the
`codex` to `cobblestone` rename is a slow one and this is the first piece.

## Smoke test 2 — codex-zig-transpiler: does the fixed point hold?

**What this repo proves, and it is a different question:** the emitter emits
the same bytes for its own source whether it runs on bare metal or as a native
binary. The subject is the whole compiler (72,741 lines at U56, up from
~68k), so this is where a rejected or mis-applied PR of ours would show as a
broken invariant rather than as a missing file.

Run on a NEW branch `u56-smoke` off `u55-minimal`. `master` is untouched at
`b830f5c` = `origin/master`.

**The branch keeps `generated/` attributable.** It is tracked, and a build
rewrites `codexzig-subject.codex`, `codexzig.ir` and both zig files, so those
artifacts must belong to the branch that produced them.

**It does NOT move the Rust arm's oracle, and the claim here that it did was
wrong.** `generated/codexcheck-subject.codex` -- the SPEC rust-codex-compiler
grades against -- is gitignored and built by `build_codexir.py`, not by
`build.py`. It and `generated/local/{codexir,codexcheck}` all still date from
the old pin and are consistent with each other. Repinning the Rust arm is a
deliberate act, not a side effect of building the transpiler.

The hazard is real but narrower than stated: the Rust arm reads those by PATH,
so once `build_codexir.py` runs at a new pin, the subject and the oracles move
under any Rust gate without the gate saying so.

### RED, and it is the shape we were looking for

The build gets through five stages — U56's seed compiles the 3.48 MB subject
(72,741 lines) to 12.1 MB of IR, and the ring plug transpiles that to zig — and
then **`zig build-exe` fails.** No fixed point at U56.

Two independent defects, both in the zig plug's prelude.

#### 1. Half of PR 131 landed, and it is the half that does not matter alone

    codexzig.qemu.zig:25076: error: zig plug: no address-of for codexzig.qemu.IRPat
    codexzig.qemu.zig:25076: error: zig plug: no address-of for codexzig.qemu.IRActStmt

`cx_address_of` switches on pointers, slices, ints, bools and BARE enums, and
`@compileError`s on anything else. `lcopy-pat` asks it for an `IRPat`:

    lcopy-pat : IRPat, MemoCtx -> IRPat
    lcopy-pat (p) (mc) =
     if address-of p < mc.mc-floor then p

PR 131 had two halves. **Half A** — a source literal lives in `.rodata` and the
displacement is SIGNED, so it answers a distinct negative instead of colliding
with the 0 sentinel — **is in U56**, prose and all. **Half B** — box every
payload-carrying variant, so `address-of` is TOTAL the way it is on bare metal
— **is not**:

    zig-typedef-boxed-loop        U55: 0    our 422405d0: 5    U56: 0
    "no longer only the self-recursive"  U55: 0    ours: 1     U56: 0

U56 is byte-for-byte U55 here. Half B is the half that makes the plug agree
with bare metal's model rather than diverge from it: bare metal boxes EVERY
variant value, which is why `address-of` there is `emit-identity-builtin` and no
type can fail it. This plug boxed only what zig's finite-size rule forced, so
`address-of`'s meaning depended on a decision made for SIZING — `IRExpr` held
`IRExpr` directly and was boxed, `IRPat` reached itself only through
`List IRPat` and was not.

Our own emitter note recorded the cost the last time: **six of fourteen rungs
fail at U55, every one on this refusal.** The gap is old; U55's memory campaign
added `lcopy-pat`/`lcopy-stmts` and made it reachable.

#### 2. A new U56 defect, ours nowhere in it

    codexzig.qemu.zig:24778: error: local variable shadows declaration of 'cx_new'

`cx_new` is the prelude's boxing helper, `fn cx_new(v: anytype) *@TypeOf(v)`.
U56 added a `cx_real_to_text` prelude fragment that declares a local
`var cx_new`, and zig forbids shadowing a file-scope declaration.

    local `var cx_new = ` in the prelude    U55: 0    our 422405d0: 0    U56: 1

**Two prelude parts that are each correct alone and collide when both are
emitted.** The prelude is tree-shaken, so this needs a program that both shows
a Real and boxes something — which is most programs, but perhaps not the ones
their gate runs.

#### What this says about their gate

The release note says "all 40 lenses streaming, so the whole compiler emits
through each". Emission is not compilation: the plug emitted this zig happily,
and `zig build-exe` is what refused. If their gate stops at emitted text, both
defects are invisible to it and neither is a surprise on their side.

Neither is a finding to send yet -- what is measured here is that OUR build of
THEIR compiler does not link. The next step is to reproduce each on a small
program, which is what makes it a report rather than a story.

## Working toward a fixed point: the u57-candidate branch

**The process is reversed this cycle, and deliberately.** Before U56 we worked
on one branch per fix and periodically cherry-picked them onto a candidate.
Now the candidate comes FIRST and PR branches are split out of it as needed.
The old order meant the candidate was always a reconstruction of work done
somewhere else, and a reconstruction can differ from what was measured.

Two branches, one per repo, each with its own job:

    Cobblestone       u57-candidate   worktree cobblestone-u57, base 6cd2ca1b
    codex-zig-transpiler  u57-candidate   base u56-smoke

`cobblestone-u56rel` stays DETACHED at the release as an untouched reference.
Turning a directory named `u56rel` into a `u57` branch is exactly the
"directory name lies about the branch" trap already on the record.

The transpiler needs its own branch because `generated/` is tracked and its
artifacts must be attributable to the candidate rather than to the release.
`master` there is untouched at `b830f5c` = `origin/master`.

### 56658c1a — box every payload-carrying variant

A cherry-pick of our own `9cc2052b`, clean, one file, +34/-13. It applied
without conflict because its author had already expressed it against the
single-file `ZigEmitter.codex` and said so in the commit message: "On our own
branch this lives across three emitter pages; the four-page split is not
upstream and is deliberately not part of this candidate." Work done carefully
five days ago cost nothing to reuse today.

### 821e650d — the real-to-text prelude shadows the boxing helper

New, because the defect is new. The fragment declared a local `var cx_new` for
the fractional part of a Real, shadowing `fn cx_new(v: anytype)`. Renamed to
`cx_frac`. Checked first that the line carries no `ShakeUse "cx_new"`, so no
real reference to the helper was touched.

**Then swept the whole prelude for the same class**: 105 file-scope
declarations against 20 distinct locals, no other collision. That defect class
is closed rather than merely this instance of it.

## The fixed point holds at u57-candidate

    pass 1  the emitter under QEMU:  2,948,357 bytes
    pass 2  the emitter as a binary: 2,948,357 bytes
    HOLDS: byte-identical
    arith: all 9 expected lines

Two fixes were sufficient; nothing was hiding behind the first error.

Run 2, behind the prose trim, holds at the same byte count -- and
`codexzig.ir`, `codexzig.qemu.zig` and `codexzig.native.zig` are byte-identical
to run 1. Only the bundled source, its blobs and the line numbers in
`ringplug.cdx.diags` move.

**That corrects a prediction made in this log an hour earlier.** The reasoning
was that a comment block shifts every span after it and spans ride in the IR,
so the emitted bytes must move. They do not. A comment-only change to
`ZigEmitter.codex` is inert below the source bundle. The prediction was wrong
and the run is what says so; the run was worth making for the provenance story
regardless.

`PROVENANCE` names `e941ba11`, clean, matching the worktree HEAD, unmoved
through the build.

### Coordinates of this claim

Zig · the compiler itself · u57-candidate · frontend-through-plug. It says
nothing about the Rust arm, nothing about safari, and nothing about U56 as
released -- where the same build fails at `zig build-exe`.

## What Update 56 did and did not take

Of the 25 commits on our `422405d0` candidate, **exactly one is missing from
U56**: `9cc2052b`, box every payload-carrying variant. Everything else landed,
adapted rather than copied.

A one-line marker heuristic flagged four; three were false positives from
reworded prose and were checked individually before being dismissed --
`688c6138` (the signed displacement) and `1f0a2265` (the `hosted-kind` guard at
`TypeChecker.codex:2716`) and `c053cd7a` (`poke-byte`, `__memset`) are all in
the release.

PR 131 carried two halves and only one landed. The half that did not is the one
that makes `address-of` total.

## Adversarial pass: what each green thing cannot see

### Nothing upstream compiles the zig its plug emits

    grep -rn 'zig build-exe|zig build |zig cc|zig test' \
      --include='*.ps1' --include='*.py' --include='*.sh' \
      --include='*.md' --include='*.codex' .        ->  no matches

The zig toolchain is invoked NOWHERE in the Cobblestone repo.
`codex/plugs/zig/run.ps1` runs the plug to produce `.zig` text and stops.

The release note says "all 40 lenses streaming, so the whole compiler emits
through each". That is a claim about EMISSION. Both defects that stop the U56
build here -- a `@compileError` for `address-of` on a payload-carrying variant,
and a local shadowing the prelude's `cx_new` -- are errors only the zig
compiler raises, so they are invisible to their suite **by construction**, not
by oversight. It also explains how the boxing half of PR 131 could fall out
with nothing on their side noticing.

This is measured, and it is the strongest candidate to send.

### What our own green instruments cannot see

- **The fixed point is self-consistency, not correctness.** Both passes share
  upstream's frontend, so a frontend defect is invisible to it -- the property
  that retired the ladder. It never validates an Update, and saying "the fixed
  point holds at u57-candidate" earlier in this log came close to implying it
  does.
- **A build that compiles is not a right answer.** The boxing fix makes
  `cx_address_of` ANSWER where it used to refuse. `usedfor address-of` over the
  U57 compiler: 94 call sites, 65 chapters, and **37 of them feed `cons-mix`**
  -- memo keys, where a wrong address collides silently rather than failing.
  That is the shape of the recorded defect where a bad `.rodata` answer made
  six distinct effect labels into one.
- **`native.sh` borrows a two-day-old oracle.** It reads
  `generated/local/codexir`, which is still at the old pin, so it would compare
  against Sep 6 while every name in sight says u57-candidate.

The arm that CAN see the boxing question is bare metal, where `address-of` is
the identity and cannot fail. `verify_fib_with_qemu.py` diffs its IR against
the native tool's; its own docstring records that comparison catching the
plug-built compiler typing every comparison `error` where bare metal said
`boolean` -- which an output check of 55 and 610 passed both ways round.

